    "book": "1-thessalonians",
    "chapter": 5,
    "reflection": "“Pray without ceasing” doesn't mean spending twenty-four hours a day speaking uninterrupted words.\n\nIt describes a life continually oriented toward God.\n\nPrayer can accompany ordinary moments.\n\nBefore a meeting.\n\nWhile travelling.\n\nDuring school drop-off.\n\nBefore responding to a difficult message.\n\nWhile making a business decision.\n\nWhen someone comes to mind.\n\nWhen gratitude suddenly rises within you.\n\nWhen anxiety begins to take over.\n\nYou don't have to wait for a perfect environment to acknowledge God's presence.\n\nDedicated prayer time remains valuable, but communion with God does not have to end when you leave your quiet place.\n\nInvite Him into the ordinary.\n\nThe more frequently you turn your heart toward God, the less likely prayer becomes merely an emergency response.\n\nIt becomes a way of life.",
