    "book": "john",
    "chapter": 1,
    "reflection": "“The Word became flesh, and lived among us.”\n\nThe eternal Word did not merely appear human.\n\nHe became flesh.\n\nJohn tells us that the One who existed before creation entered creation and dwelt among ordinary people.\n\nThis is astonishing humility.\n\nThe Creator entered the limitations of human life. The One worthy of heaven's worship walked dusty roads, sat at tables, touched the sick and welcomed people society overlooked.\n\nJesus reveals that greatness in God's Kingdom does not resemble worldly obsession with status.\n\nThe incarnation confronts our desire always to be impressive.\n\nChrist humbled Himself.\n\nSo following Jesus will sometimes lead us toward places the world considers beneath us: serving unnoticed, caring for inconvenient people, choosing obedience over applause.\n\nIf the Son of God was willing to come near, we cannot build Christian lives around remaining comfortably distant from people's needs.",
    "declaration": "Jesus humbled Himself and came near. I will follow His example by choosing humility, compassion and faithful service rather than living for status and recognition.",
    "prayer": "Jesus, I marvel that You became flesh and lived among us. Deliver me from pride and the need to appear important. Give me Your humility and teach me to notice, love and serve the people You place around me. Amen."
