    "reflection": "“I wait for Yahweh. My soul waits. I hope in his word.”\n\nBiblical hope is not vague optimism.\n\nIt is expectation anchored in God.\n\nThe psalmist compares waiting for God to watchmen waiting for morning.\n\nA watchman cannot make sunrise happen faster.\n\nBut he knows morning will come.\n\nSome seasons of faith are like that.\n\nYou cannot manufacture the answer.\n\nYou cannot control another person's choices.\n\nYou cannot accelerate every process.\n\nBut you can place your hope in God's Word.\n\nHope says:\n\n“I may not know exactly what God will do in this circumstance, but I know He remains faithful.”\n\nThat protects us from attaching our entire hope to one specific outcome.\n\nOur deepest hope is not:\n\nEverything will happen exactly as I want.\n\nIt is:\n\nGod will remain God, and I can trust Him.",
