    "reflection": "James writes:\n\n“Count it all joy, my brothers, when you fall into various temptations, knowing that the testing of your faith produces endurance.”\n\nHe does not say pain itself is pleasant.\n\nJoy comes from recognising what God can produce through tested faith.\n\nTrials reveal whether our trust has roots.\n\nThey develop endurance that cannot be learned merely by reading about endurance.\n\nThis does not mean we need to celebrate injustice, abuse, illness or loss as though evil were good.\n\nWe can grieve what is painful while trusting God to work in us through it.\n\nJoy and sorrow are not always opposites in Christian life.\n\nSometimes they coexist.\n\nYou can say:\n\n“This hurts—and God is still at work.”",
