    "book": "colossians",
    "chapter": 3,
    "reflection": "“Even as Christ forgave you, so you also do.”\n\nForgiveness is one of Christianity's most demanding commands because it deals with genuine wounds.\n\nForgiveness does not mean pretending the offence did not matter.\n\nIt does not necessarily mean immediate restoration of trust.\n\nIt does not remove appropriate boundaries or consequences.\n\nAnd reconciliation requires more than one person's forgiveness; it involves truth and, where necessary, repentance.\n\nForgiveness means releasing personal vengeance into God's hands and refusing to nurture hatred as a permanent home.\n\nWhy forgive?\n\nBecause forgiven people have received extraordinary mercy.\n\nThat does not make the wound small.\n\nIt makes God's grace greater.\n\nSometimes forgiveness is a decision you must reaffirm repeatedly as emotions heal.\n\nBegin with willingness.",
