    "slug": "restore-gently",
    "title": "Restore Gently",
    "theme": "Gentleness toward failure",
    "movement": "Fruitful Living",
    "scriptureReference": "Galatians 6:1–2",
    "book": "galatians",
    "chapter": 6,
    "reflection": "Paul tells believers that when someone is caught in wrongdoing, those who are spiritual should restore that person “in a spirit of gentleness.”\n\nSpiritual maturity is revealed partly by how we treat people who fail.\n\nDo we enjoy exposing them?\n\nGossip about them?\n\nUse their weakness to make ourselves look superior?\n\nOr do we care about restoration?\n\nGentleness does not mean ignoring serious wrongdoing.\n\nTruth matters.\n\nAccountability matters.\n\nConsequences may be necessary.\n\nBut the goal should not be humiliation.\n\nPaul also warns the person restoring another to watch herself.\n\nWhy?\n\nBecause none of us is beyond temptation.\n\nHumility remembers:\n\n“I also need grace.”",
    "declaration": "When others fail, I will pursue truth and appropriate accountability without delighting in humiliation. I choose restoration, humility and gentleness.",
    "prayer": "Father, protect me from self-righteousness. Give me wisdom when responding to another person's failure and help me carry truth with humility and restorative love. Amen.\n\nSELF-CONTROL"
