    "book": "proverbs",
    "chapter": 31,
    "reflection": "“She considers a field, and buys it. With the fruit of her hands, she plants a vineyard.”\n\nThis woman is economically active.\n\nShe considers before she purchases.\n\nShe evaluates.\n\nDecides.\n\nInvests.\n\nProduces.\n\nThat word considers matters.\n\nBiblical enterprise is not reckless ambition.\n\nWisdom comes before investment.\n\nThe passage also challenges the idea that business, property ownership or commercial activity is somehow incompatible with godly womanhood.\n\nThis woman fears the Lord—and she conducts business.\n\nMoney is not her god.\n\nEnterprise is not her identity.\n\nBut economic wisdom is part of her stewardship.\n\nWhether you run a company, develop a career, manage household resources, invest, create products or are simply learning financial wisdom, the principle applies:\n\nconsider before committing.\n\nResearch.\n\nCount the cost.\n\nSeek wisdom.\n\nUnderstand risk.\n\nPray.\n\nThen act decisively when appropriate.",
