    "slug": "the-god-who-sees-you",
    "title": "The God Who Sees You",
    "theme": "God sees the overlooked",
    "movement": "Women of the Bible",
    "scriptureReference": "Genesis 16:7–13",
    "book": "genesis",
    "chapter": 16,
    "reflection": "Hagar was caught in a painful situation shaped by the decisions and power of others.\n\nShe fled into the wilderness.\n\nThere, God met her.\n\nShe responded by calling Him:\n\n“the God who sees me.”\n\nHagar's story matters because Scripture does not reserve divine attention for the socially powerful.\n\nGod saw an Egyptian servant woman in distress.\n\nThe overlooked woman was not overlooked by Him.\n\nYou may know what it feels like to occupy the less powerful position in a situation.\n\nTo have people discuss your life without hearing your voice.\n\nTo carry pain created by other people's decisions.\n\nHagar reminds us that God's sight reaches where human attention does not.\n\nBeing seen does not mean every complication vanishes immediately.\n\nBut it means you are not invisible within it.",
