    "chapter": 1,
    "reflection": "Luke describes Elizabeth and Zechariah as righteous before God.\n\nThen he tells us they had no child.\n\nThat combination matters.\n\nTheir unanswered longing was not evidence that they had failed God.\n\nSometimes we quietly assume:\n\n“If I were more faithful, perhaps this would have happened by now.”\n\nElizabeth's story challenges that equation.\n\nFaithful people can experience long disappointment.\n\nEventually God intervened extraordinarily in her particular story, and John the Baptist was born.\n\nBut the deeper lesson is not that every long wait will end with the exact outcome we desire.\n\nIt is that delay is not reliable evidence of divine rejection.\n\nYour unanswered question does not automatically tell you what God thinks of you.\n\nKeep walking faithfully.",
    "declaration": "Delay does not mean God has forgotten me. I refuse to measure His love by the speed with which my circumstances change.",
    "prayer": "Father, strengthen me where waiting has become exhausting. Protect me from interpreting delay as rejection and help me remain faithful while entrusting the outcome to You. Amen."
