    "slug": "what-was-meant-for-evil",
    "title": "What Was Meant for Evil",
    "theme": "Providence and redemption",
    "movement": "Hope, Perseverance & Eternity",
    "scriptureReference": "Genesis 50:15–21",
    "book": "genesis",
    "chapter": 50,
    "reflection": "Joseph told his brothers:\n\n“As for you, you meant evil against me, but God meant it for good.”\n\nJoseph does not pretend his brothers' actions were good.\n\nThey intended evil.\n\nThat distinction matters.\n\nGod's sovereignty never requires us to rename evil as righteousness.\n\nBut evil does not possess equal sovereignty with God.\n\nJoseph's betrayal became part of a story through which lives were eventually preserved.\n\nThat does not excuse his brothers.\n\nIt reveals God's astonishing ability to redeem what human beings meant for destruction.\n\nSome painful things in your story may never make complete sense to you.\n\nDo not force an explanation Scripture has not given.\n\nBut you can know this:\n\nHuman evil does not have enough authority to make God helpless.\n\nHe remains able to redeem.",
    "declaration": "I will call evil what it is while trusting God's greater power to redeem what others intended for harm.",
    "prayer": "Father, redeem the painful parts of my story according to Your wisdom. Keep me from bitterness, false explanations and despair, and show Your goodness even where others acted wrongly. Amen.\n\nYellow-flag audit result\n\nWe now have:\n\nKEEP: James/trials progression\nREFINE: Day 340 title\nKEEP + safeguard: forgiveness material\nKEEP: Luke 16 applications\nKEEP: Purpose/Generosity distinction\nKEEP + sharpen: justification sequence\nREPLACE: Day 337\nREPLACE: Day 343\n\nThat substantially improves the final movement and reduces the overdependence on Romans 8 without sacrificing its major doctrines.\n\nOne issue remains before the theological audit\n\nThe next pass should be Scripture accuracy and theological precision, and this one deserves care.\n\nWe should examine language around:\n\nthe Trinity, Holy Spirit, salvation, grace and works, justification, sanctification, glorification, spiritual warfare, healing, suffering, giving/prosperity, women's biblical examples, marriage/parenting, purpose/calling, and eternity.\n\nThe goal is not to flatten denominational differences. Where faithful Christians legitimately disagree, the devotional should avoid presenting a secondary interpretation as though it were the gospel itself.\n\nOnce that passes, we will be very close to calling the YEAR365 manuscript editorially frozen."
